1
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:21,067
NovaPay's web app calls all 24 Meridian APIs through Tyk Gateway: 23 refuse it
The one that answers, Payments, allows every website

2
00:00:21,667 --> 00:00:27,033
Meridian runs 24 APIs, and each keeps its own copy of the CORS rules
No single place says what the rule is

3
00:00:27,633 --> 00:00:34,733
Payments' copy allows every website
It was opened up during an incident and never closed

4
00:00:58,333 --> 00:01:03,667
Tyk 5.16: create a Fragment and paste in the bank's CORS standard
It is stored once, under Assets in Tyk Dashboard

5
00:01:04,667 --> 00:01:13,100
Name it. APIs will point at it as fragment://cors-browser-apps
Saved. No API uses it yet

6
00:01:24,833 --> 00:01:30,367
All 24 APIs now reference the one Fragment
And the Dashboard lists every API that uses it

7
00:01:31,367 --> 00:01:42,300
Monday's launch: add NovaPay's origin to the Fragment
One edit, one save

8
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:51,067
The same page, reloaded: all 24 APIs accept NovaPay
No API was edited or redeployed

9
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:59,633
Payments now follows the standard: the wildcard is gone
The setting is read-only in the API and names its Fragment

10
00:02:00,233 --> 00:02:11,367
A mistake: a value of the wrong type
Tyk checks all 24 APIs first and refuses the save

